title: Ghidra MCP Development Rules

rules:
  critical:
    - push back when user idea is wrong, user NOT always right
    - NEVER assume APIs/module shapes/Ghidra signatures you dont know, verify via docs or inspection
    - smallest possible change, no refactoring unless asked
    - ALWAYS explore existing src/ BEFORE writing anything, reuse helpers, NEVER reimplement
    - if a helper/pattern exists elsewhere, import/extract it, NEVER copy-paste or recreate
    - run `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` + `cargo fmt --check` + `cargo test` after every Rust change
    - run `mvn -q compile` after every Java change, `mvn -q package` before shipping the zip
    - NEVER commit/push without explicit user instruction
    - NEVER commit design specs/plans/notes to the repo
    - NEVER edit .env, never log secrets, cookies, tokens
  code:
    - DELETE dead code, never comment it out
    - ZERO comments — no //, no ///, no //!, no /* */, no Javadoc — names must carry intent
    - exception for comments only non-obvious invariants, cross-language constraints, WHY not WHAT
    - less code = smarter code, same functionality fewer lines always wins
    - if it can be simpler, make it simpler, no clever, no cute, just clear
    - NEVER overengineer, NEVER speculative abstractions, NEVER anticipate imaginary requirements
    - NEVER wrapper functions that add nothing, NEVER helpers for one-time operations
    - NEVER backwards-compat shims, feature flags, or dead-code fallbacks — just change the code
    - NEVER add error handling/validation for scenarios that cannot happen (trust internal callers)
    - validate ONLY at system boundaries (HTTP input, CLI args, env vars)
    - inline single-use vars, hoist statics out of loops
    - no magic numbers, unused imports, dead functions, unused types
    - DRY — if a pattern repeats 2+ times, extract it
    - deduplicate ruthlessly, prefer importing over reimplementing
    - KISS, flat over nested, early returns, guard clauses at top, max 3 nesting levels
    - every code path returns a value, no empty catch/match arms
    - never trust snapshots across .await / across HTTP boundary, re-query state
  rust:
    critical:
      - edition 2024, latest stable toolchain
      - NEVER .unwrap() / .expect() in library code, all fallible ops return Result
      - NEVER anyhow in library crates, typed errors via thiserror
      - NEVER println!/eprintln!, use tracing (info!/warn!/error!/debug!/trace!)
      - NEVER sleep loops, use tokio::time::interval
      - `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` must pass with zero warnings
      - `cargo fmt` enforced, never reformat unrelated code
    style:
      - let-else for early unwrap, never `if let Some(x) = ... { long-block } else { bail }`
      - `#[must_use]` on builders, constructors, pure functions returning values worth checking
      - `#[non_exhaustive]` on all public enums/structs (forward compat without SemVer break)
      - `#[default]` on enums with an obvious default variant
      - precise captures `use<>` syntax on async returns when needed
      - prefer `&str` over `String` in function params, `Cow<'_, str>` when ownership is conditional
      - `impl Trait` in arg position for one-shot callbacks, generics only when reused
      - never `.clone()` where `&`/`Arc`/move suffices, clippy::redundant_clone catches most
      - derive `Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash` only when actually needed, not reflexively
      - newtype IDs via `define_id!` macro or tuple struct, never raw String/u64 for identifiers
      - `#[serde(skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")]` on optional request fields
      - `#[serde(flatten)] extra: serde_json::Map<String, Value>` on response structs for forward compat
      - `Other(String)` variant on enums parsing upstream values to survive unknown additions
    perf:
      - zero-copy streaming, `Pin<Box<dyn Stream>>`, `Bytes` over `Vec<u8>`
      - shared `reqwest::Client` with pool, never new client per request
      - `SmallVec<[T; N]>` for hot paths with known-small size
      - `Arc<str>` over `Arc<String>`, `Box<str>` over `String` when immutable
      - `hashbrown::HashMap` or `ahash` for non-crypto maps (std HashMap is SipHash, slow)
      - `OnceCell`/`LazyLock` for init-once globals, never Mutex-guarded init
      - release profile: lto=true, codegen-units=1, strip=true, panic=abort, opt-level=3
      - profile before optimizing, never guess, use `cargo flamegraph` or `samply`
      - avoid `format!` in hot paths, use `write!` into reused buffer
      - iterator chains compile to tight loops, prefer them over explicit for when readable
    async:
      - `#[tokio::main(flavor = "multi_thread")]` for servers, `current_thread` for CLIs
      - never block in async fn, offload to `spawn_blocking`
      - `select!` with `biased;` when ordering matters, else default for fairness
      - cancellation via `tokio_util::sync::CancellationToken`, never ad-hoc AtomicBool
      - bounded channels (`mpsc::channel(N)`) over unbounded, backpressure matters
      - never hold `Mutex` across `.await`, use `tokio::sync::Mutex` or restructure
    errors:
      - typed errors per crate with `thiserror::Error`
      - `#[from]` for transparent conversion, `#[source]` for wrapping
      - library errors never panic, application `main` may `.context()` and print
      - propagate with `?`, never .unwrap() the Err branch
    patterns:
      - builder pattern for requests with 3+ optional fields, `#[must_use]` on each setter
      - enum-based state machines over boolean flags
      - typestate pattern where compile-time state matters
  java:
    critical:
      - target JDK 21, no older syntax, compile with --release 21
      - Msg.info/warn/error (Ghidra logger) or SLF4J, NEVER System.out/err, NEVER printStackTrace
      - NEVER checked exceptions in public API, wrap into RuntimeException at boundary
      - every HTTP handler wraps in try/catch and returns a meaningful status
      - no mutable static state, use final fields + constructor injection
    style:
      - records for request/response DTOs, never getter/setter POJOs
      - sealed interfaces for closed hierarchies (result types, command variants)
      - pattern matching in switch expressions for sum types
      - `var` for locals where RHS is obvious, never `var` on API boundaries
      - text blocks (`"""`) for multi-line strings
      - Stream API for transforms, collectors over manual loops
      - Optional for return types that may be empty, NEVER for fields or params
      - Map.of / List.of / Set.of for small immutables, never new HashMap with .put chain
    perf:
      - fixed thread-pool executor sized `Math.max(4, availableProcessors())` for HTTP
      - daemon threads only (`setDaemon(true)`) — never block JVM shutdown
      - StringBuilder for concat in loops, never `+` in hot paths
      - avoid autoboxing in tight loops, primitive collections if ever needed (Eclipse Collections / HPPC)
      - `String.join` over manual delimiter handling
      - pre-size `ArrayList`/`HashMap` when bounds known
    ghidra:
      - all Ghidra API access on appropriate thread, wrap mutations in `Program.startTransaction`/`endTransaction`
      - commit transactions in finally, `program.endTransaction(tid, success)`
      - obtain `DecompInterface` once per request, dispose after
      - respect `TaskMonitor.isCancelled()` in long scans
      - never cache `Program` across requests, fetch fresh via `ProgramManager`
    errors:
      - plain-text responses with HTTP status matching error class (400 input, 404 missing, 500 server)
      - include enough detail to debug but NEVER stack traces in response body
      - Msg.error with full exception for server-side logs
  performance:
    - blazing fast, lightweight, zero-cost abstractions, minimal allocations
    - profile before optimizing, measure don't guess
    - connection pooling shared across requests
    - batch reads, batch writes, never interleave blocking calls
    - cache computed values with known lifetime, evict eagerly
    - no unbounded growth in long-lived processes, clear listeners/timers/handles
  security:
    - HTTP plugin binds to loopback by default, require explicit opt-in for wider bind
    - no auth: never expose to untrusted networks
    - validate addresses/names at handler entry, reject empty / malformed early
    - URL-decode once, never twice (double-decode = smuggling risk)
    - no string interpolation into shell commands, always parameterize
    - no secrets in code/config/logs, env vars only
    - no dynamic code execution from user input
  architecture:
    - two components: Rust `ghidra-mcp` binary (MCP stdio), Java plugin (Ghidra-embedded HTTP)
    - Rust crate is independently usable as an MCP bridge for any GhidraMCP-compatible HTTP server
    - shared contract: plain-text HTTP, newline-delimited records, form-encoded POST bodies
    - Rust errors: `BridgeError` enum, mapped to `rmcp::ErrorData` at handler boundary
    - Java errors: HTTP status + plain-text body, logged via Msg
    - tools in Rust: `#[tool]` + typed `Parameters<T>` structs, never raw `Value`
    - endpoints in Java: single router table, never inline duplicate offset/limit parsing
  forbidden:
    - empty catch blocks
    - `println!`/`eprintln!` (use tracing) / `System.out.println` (use Msg)
    - `.unwrap()`/`.expect()` in non-test code
    - checked exceptions bubbling through public Java API
    - commented-out code, TODO without a tracking issue
    - single-use wrappers (1-3 lines called once, inline it)
    - feature flags, backwards-compat shims, abstractions for one-time ops
    - redundant type annotations the compiler already infers
    - `var` in Rust where the type matters, `var` in Java on public API
  antiPatterns:
    rust:
      - "match x { Some(y) => y, None => return }" > "let Some(y) = x else { return };"
      - "if x.is_some() { x.unwrap() }" > "if let Some(v) = x"
      - "String::from(s)" > "s.to_owned()" (when s is &str)
      - "s.to_string()" > "s.into()" (when target is String and s is &str)
      - "v.iter().map(...).collect::<Vec<_>>()" > "v.iter().map(...).collect()" (let inference work)
      - "Vec::new(); for x in ... { v.push(x) }" > ".collect()" / iterator chain
      - "Arc<Mutex<HashMap<K, V>>>" > "DashMap<K, V>" (concurrent maps)
      - "format!(\"{}\", x)" > "x.to_string()" (single arg)
    java:
      - "value != null && !value.isEmpty()" > "value != null && !value.isBlank()"
      - "new HashMap<>() { {{ put(...) }} }" > "Map.of(...)"
      - "list.stream().filter(...).findFirst().get()" > "list.stream().filter(...).findFirst().orElse(null)"
      - "Integer.parseInt(s)" > guarded `parseIntOrDefault` when input is untrusted
      - "for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++)" > "for (var x : list)" (when index unused)
  deps:
    - minimize deps, prefer std over external
    - every added dep must justify ≥20 LOC saved
    - prefer rustls over native-tls (no OpenSSL), reqwest features minimal (rustls-tls + http2)
    - JDK http server (com.sun.net.httpserver) is free, don't pull Jetty/Undertow
    - audit transitive deps, `cargo audit` before release
  git:
    - NEVER commit or push without explicit user permission, always ask first
    - format: `type: short imperative message`
    - types: fix, feat, chore, refactor, perf, test, docs, build, ci
    - subject ≤50 chars preferred, hard cap 72, no trailing period
    - imperative mood: "add", "fix", "remove" — never "added", "adds", "adding"
    - no body by default — skip when subject is self-explanatory
    - body ONLY for: breaking changes, security fixes, data migrations, non-obvious WHY
    - body wraps at 72 chars, bullets use `-` not `*`, WHY over WHAT
    - NEVER "this commit does X", NEVER I/we/now/currently
    - NEVER Co-Authored-By, NEVER Claude/AI attribution
    - NEVER emoji in commits or code
    - NEVER --amend a published commit, create a new commit
    - NEVER --force push to shared branches
    - group related files into one commit, don't split atomic changes
  testing:
    - unit tests alongside implementation in Rust (`#[cfg(test)] mod tests`)
    - integration tests for HTTP contract in `tests/` dir
    - test names describe behavior: `decompile_returns_error_when_function_missing`
    - never test private implementation details, only public behavior
    - fast tests only in default `cargo test`, slow/integration behind `--features integration`
